to save partially completed forms. It measured success not by the number of services placed online, but by the share of users able to complete them without avoidable failure.\\n\\nQuestion: What broader principle does the passage support?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"A service is inclusive once a website exists.\",\"hi\":\"A service is inclusive once a website exists.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Complex language improves public trust.\",\"hi\":\"Complex language improves public trust.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Access should be judged by practical usability, not availability alone.\",\"hi\":\"Access should be judged by practical usability, not availability alone.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Offline assistance prevents digital progress.\",\"hi\":\"Offline assistance prevents digital progress.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"cbb9b8fd-d021-4e12-b1e5-b74ad3a8be88\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: Which statement best captures the author’s argument?\",\"hi\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: Which statement best captures the author’s argument?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Repair access can reduce waste without ignoring legitimate safeguards.\",\"hi\":\"Repair access can reduce waste without ignoring legitimate safeguards.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Environmental goals require the removal of all safety rules.\",\"hi\":\"Environmental goals require the removal of all safety rules.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Intellectual property is unrelated to repair policy.\",\"hi\":\"Intellectual property is unrelated to repair policy.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Convenience for manufacturers should be the only standard.\",\"hi\":\"Convenience for manufacturers should be the only standard.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"bdcf0bf3-a6f5-4961-b326-9000cb2c83dc\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: What problem does the editorial identify?\",\"hi\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: What problem does the editorial identify?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Whole devices are often replaced because a small part fails.\",\"hi\":\"Whole devices are often replaced because a small part fails.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Consumers are legally forced to repair forever.\",\"hi\":\"Consumers are legally forced to repair forever.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Spare parts always create safety risks.\",\"hi\":\"Spare parts always create safety risks.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Manufacturers provide too much diagnostic information.\",\"hi\":\"Manufacturers provide too much diagnostic information.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"9eabc36f-3489-42c1-9cc7-0e5102a00587\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: What is the stated goal?\",\"hi\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: What is the stated goal?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"To require endless repair of every product\",\"hi\":\"To require endless repair of every product\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"To abolish product replacement completely\",\"hi\":\"To abolish product replacement completely\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"To transfer all repair work to consumers\",\"hi\":\"To transfer all repair work to consumers\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"To make safe and economical repair a practical choice\",\"hi\":\"To make safe and economical repair a practical choice\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"95b7d07c-8e88-4e70-b917-def7cd7bc550\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Report-Style Passage 1: Urban Heat Planning\\n\\nA city review of summer preparedness found that heat risk was not evenly distributed. Neighbourhoods with little shade and large paved areas stayed warmer for longer, while outdoor workers faced repeated exposure during peak afternoon hours. The review recommended cool-roof programmes, shaded bus stops, drinking-water points and work schedules that avoid the hottest period. It also warned that planting trees alone would not provide immediate relief because young trees take time to create shade. The report therefore favoured a mix of short-term protection and long-term design.\\n\\nQuestion: Why did the report not rely only on tree planting?\",\"hi\":\"Original Report-Style Passage 1: Urban Heat Planning\\n\\nA city review of summer preparedness found that heat risk was not evenly distributed. Neighbourhoods with little shade and large paved areas stayed warmer for longer, while outdoor workers faced repeated exposure during peak afternoon hours. The review recommended cool-roof programmes, shaded bus stops, drinking-water points and work schedules that avoid the hottest period. It also warned that planting trees alone would not provide immediate relief because young trees take time to create shade. The report therefore favoured a mix of short-term protection and long-term design.\\n\\nQuestion: Why did the report not rely only on tree planting?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Trees always increase urban temperature.\",\"hi\":\"Trees always increase urban temperature.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Residents refused every planting proposal.\",\"hi\":\"Residents refused every planting proposal.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Trees cannot survive in any city.\",\"hi\":\"Trees cannot survive in any city.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"New trees take time to provide substantial shade.\",\"hi\":\"New trees take time to provide substantial shade.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"5747d4b1-efcc-4499-83f7-04fc76c6de3e\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 2: AI Literacy in Schools\\n\\nDebate about artificial intelligence in schools often focuses on whether students should use particular tools. A more durable question is whether students can evaluate automated output. They need to recognise unsupported claims, hidden assumptions and confident language that lacks evidence. This form of literacy does not require accepting every new system, nor does it justify banning technology without examination. Schools should teach students to compare outputs with reliable sources, disclose when assistance was used and remain responsible for the final work.\\n\\nQuestion: What does the editorial consider the more durable question?\",\"hi\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 2: AI Literacy in Schools\\n\\nDebate about artificial intelligence in schools often focuses on whether students should use particular tools. A more durable question is whether students can evaluate automated output. They need to recognise unsupported claims, hidden assumptions and confident language that lacks evidence. This form of literacy does not require accepting every new system, nor does it justify banning technology without examination. Schools should teach students to compare outputs with reliable sources, disclose when assistance was used and remain responsible for the final work.\\n\\nQuestion: What does the editorial consider the more durable question?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Whether every school can buy the same software\",\"hi\":\"Whether every school can buy the same software\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Whether students type faster than teachers\",\"hi\":\"Whether students type faster than teachers\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\